{
  "name": "Monthly OPM hours and activity driver surveys",
  "change": "Pre-fill the OPM hours survey from last month, make the commercial-team split required, and take driver counts from standard reports instead of the spreadsheet.",
  "reason": "Two of the three changes already exist in the mail (FTE and commercial-team rollover, a prior-month hours report, and the standing offer to accept standard reports instead of the driver survey), and the 1,630 minutes a month rests on a padded headcount (the 50 on 2080.eml include cc'd directors and the OPM staff), a skipped-month frequency, and guessed per-person and chase figures.",
  "research": "Find mail showing how many managers actually fill in the hours survey each month, how long it takes them, how many RCs took up the report option and why the others did not, and how often the OPM team has to chase missing commercial-team splits; a proposal limited to the required-split check, or to pre-filling the hours themselves, might hold up on that evidence."
}

{
  "name": "Calpine daily gas nomination handling",
  "change": "Replace Calpine's emailed Word nomination, phone call and confirmation email with a structured nomination that rolls forward and needs a reply only when it changes.",
  "reason": "The mail shows only that Calpine emails a Word nomination on weekdays; the phone call on every day, the HPL confirmation loop, the re-keying and the daily-including-weekends rate are not supported, and the estimate counts Calpine's own time, so the 13 minutes a day is not realistic.",
  "research": "Look in the schedulers' mailboxes (Robert Lloyd, Aimee Lannou, Juliann Kemp) for what HPL does with the Calpine nomination: replies or confirmations sent to Calpine, keying errors or corrections, or revised nominations that caused rework. Evidence like that could support a smaller weekday-only saving for the Enron scheduler."
}

{
  "name": "Strangers Gas write-off approval memos",
  "change": "Write off tiny Strangers Gas volumes under a standing approval and send the rest to Daren Farmer as one weekly electronic list instead of a paper memo per meter.",
  "reason": "The memos are a one-time cleanup of 1999-2000 flows sent mostly in a burst from 11 Dec 2000 to 12 Jan 2001 and none appear after that, though the mailbox runs to late 2001; so the work is an episode that has ended, not 10 items a month, and the same work has already been rejected in decisions.md for this reason.",
  "research": "Look for Strangers Gas write-off memos about current-month flow after January 2001, in this or other mailboxes (Cernosek, Lannou, Boas), that show small items keep coming at a steady rate."
}

{
  "name": "Monthly sweep of Houston books for Enron Canada (ECC) deals before NYMEX close",
  "change": "Book ECC counterparty deals into the FT-US/CAND-ERMS book automatically, and run a report that lists any that slip through, instead of a monthly manual sweep and email chain.",
  "reason": "The same work and the same change are already saved as decisions.md item 3, 'Monthly search of Houston books for Enron Canada (ECC) deals' ($364.65 a month), which cites six of the same eight Giron messages (114, 195, 360, 407, 535, 550).",
  "research": null
}

{
  "name": "Monthly GLO/EGM gas-for-crude swap tickets",
  "change": "Book the HPL-to-GLO sale as a standing zero-price ticket and price the HPL-to-ENA demand tickets by formula (firm volume x the month's index), so only the crude value is keyed monthly.",
  "reason": "The same change to the same work is already saved in decisions.md (item 7). This version also gets today's process wrong: the mail shows the monthly steps were not done at all from March 2001 on and had to be caught up months later, not keyed by hand each month before flash.",
  "research": null
}

{
  "name": "Redirecting mail sent to the wrong Bill Williams",
  "change": "Have IT add department and location to both Bill Williams directory names so senders can tell them apart when addressing.",
  "reason": "The same work and the same change are already saved as 'Correcting mail sent to the wrong Bill Williams' (item 8 in decisions.md), which uses 9 of this proposal's 16 occurrences, so saving it again would count the saving twice.",
  "research": null
}

{
  "name": "Monthly EWS admin-assistant roster check",
  "change": "Build the monthly admin-assistant list from HR/directory data and ask assistants to reply only when their record changed or is blank.",
  "reason": "The same work and change are already saved in decisions.md as item 8, 'Monthly EWS admin-assistant roster check' ($134.30 a month), which cites the same single message, williams-w3/sent_items/313.eml, so saving it again would count the saving twice.",
  "research": null
}

{
  "name": "Daily hand-forwarding of the Energy Market Report to West power traders",
  "change": "Send the vendor's daily report straight to a distribution list of the 12 traders, or forward it with an automatic mail rule, so nobody forwards it by hand.",
  "reason": "The same work and the same change are already saved as item 19 in decisions.md, 'Daily forward of the Energy Market Report to West Power traders' ($8.50 a month), which cites all seven of these messages, so saving it again would count the saving twice.",
  "research": null
}

{
  "name": "Second, unposted TW Current Shipper rate matrix",
  "today": "When the TW Current Shipper rates change, Rita Bianchi prepares the official rate matrix (MATRIX200111.doc), has it posted on the web, and Elizabeth Brown forwards it to the TW commercial team. Rita also keeps a second spreadsheet of the same Current Shipper rates and surcharges, adds a new tab for each change, and emails it separately to the same team. She says she does not know whether anyone still uses it. On 29 Oct 2001 she sent it late to Elizabeth and could not remember whether she had sent it to Jeanette Doll.",
  "change": "Ask the team once whether anyone uses the second matrix; if nobody does, stop keeping it up and sending it.",
  "explanation": "Rita asks the eight recipients once whether anyone uses the unposted spreadsheet matrix. If nobody does, she stops keeping it up. The posted matrix, which already has a table of the base reservation and commodity rates and reservation surcharges for the Global Settlement contracts, becomes the only one. If someone does need it, for example for the history the dated tabs hold, it can be kept for that person alone. Afterwards each rate change produces one matrix that is posted and forwarded once. Nothing needs to be built.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/lokay-m/inbox/7.eml",
      "quote": "Here is another matrix of TW \"Current Shipper\" rates & surcharges."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/lokay-m/inbox/7.eml",
      "quote": "I don't know whether anyone needs/uses it, anymore, but I have been keeping it up.",
      "note": "The person who does the work doubts that anyone uses it."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/lokay-m/inbox/7.eml",
      "quote": "There is a new tab for each change in Current Shipper Rates.",
      "note": "Each rate change means adding a tab and entering the new rates, and this has happened more than once."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/lokay-m/inbox/7.eml",
      "quote": "Elizabeth - I forgot to send this to you this morning.  \nJeanette - I don't recall whether I sent this along with the other TW files.",
      "note": "Sending a second file separately leads to missed sends and resends."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/lokay-m/inbox/8.eml",
      "quote": "a table of the base res. and comm. rates and reservation surcharges for the Global Settlement contracts",
      "note": "The posted matrix already has the Current Shipper rates, so the second matrix repeats them."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/lokay-m/inbox/8.eml",
      "quote": "Only the rates for the \"Current Shippers\" as defined in TW's Global Settlement changed,",
      "note": "The change that triggered the second matrix is already in the posted one."
    }
  ],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": null,
  "minutes": 20
}

{
  "name": "Second, unposted TW Current Shipper rate matrix",
  "change": "Ask the TW team once whether anyone uses Rita Bianchi's second Current Shipper rate matrix and stop keeping it up if nobody does.",
  "reason": "The work shows up only once in the archive (29 Oct 2001), and nothing says how often the Current Shipper rates change, so the quarterly or monthly rate behind the saving is a guess.",
  "research": "Look for other TW rate-matrix postings or Current Shipper rate changes, such as earlier MATRIX*.doc files, Global Settlement surcharge updates, or Rita Bianchi's or Elizabeth Brown's mail, that show how often the rates change; and for any reply saying whether anyone uses the spreadsheet."
}

{
  "name": "Manager approval of small expense reports in Concur",
  "change": "Auto-approve expense reports under $100 in Concur, with the manager reviewing a monthly digest afterwards.",
  "reason": "The five approvals in 12 days were a one-time rush to clear expenses before the group left Enron's expense system for UBSW Energy, so the work ends and the monthly rate of 12 is not real; the whole archive shows only seven such reports.",
  "research": "Look for this approval work, and how often it happens, under UBSW Energy's new expense process after the transaction closes; Enron's Concur/XMS approvals for this group stop there."
}

{
  "name": "Settling HPL day/spot deal price and volume disputes",
  "change": "Sitara sends each counterparty a next-morning recap of the prior day's HPL day deals so price and volume mismatches are fixed at once, not at invoicing.",
  "reason": "The disputes are a post-sale cleanup: HPL passed to AEP from June 2001 (136.eml, 106.eml 'a deal from when we were at Enron'), every disputed deal is for Dec 2000 to May 2001 production, the earlier mail shows only about three such threads in all of 2000, and several cited threads are not day-deal disputes, so there is no ongoing work at 5 a month for a recap to prevent.",
  "research": "Find disputes over day deals in Enron's own books after June 2001, outside HPL (for example ENA Texas or other desks' settlements mail), at a steady rate, plus evidence of whether those deals were already confirmed by Enron's Confirmations group; without that the verdict stands."
}

{
  "name": "Annual budget staffing and salary worksheet",
  "change": "Pre-fill each manager's budget staffing and salary worksheet from an HR/payroll extract by RC",
  "reason": "The mail contradicts the estimate: Diane asks managers for only 'a little bit of information', the salary step is a short list of positions with a one-word high/low guess, and she says averages are as accurate as current salaries, so a payroll pre-fill would remove only a few minutes per manager once a year, not 30.",
  "research": "Look for mail from later budget cycles (2001 for 2002) or from managers showing that the salary and roster step actually took them substantial time, needed several exchanges with Diane, or produced budget errors."
}
